.\" whytime-report(1), hand-written. cargo xtask man adds the title line and
.\" replaces each ".\" @SECTION" line with that section rendered from cli.rs.
.SH NAME
whytime\-report \- print the report of a whytime trace file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY "whytime report"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whytime report
reads a trace file written by
.B whytime \-\-record
.I FILE
and prints its report to standard error, exactly as the recording run printed
it: the same text, or with
.B \-\-json
the same JSON, byte for byte.
It runs the recorded events through the same code as a live run, so
.B \-\-timeline
and
.B \-\-perfetto
may be given even if the recording run did not use them, and produce the
output that run would have.
.PP
Replaying needs no privileges, no BPF support in the kernel and none of the
traced processes, so a trace file can be examined later or on another machine.
It must be replayed by a
.B whytime
built for the architecture it was recorded on.
.PP
.B \-\-flamegraph
and
.B \-\-folded
need the call stacks, which only a recording made with one of them contains.
For any other trace file,
.B whytime report
prints that there is no flamegraph to draw, prints the report, and exits 2.
.PP
A trace file is checked completely before anything is replayed; a file that
fails the check is rejected with the reason, never half replayed.
The reasons include:
.TP
.B not a whytime trace file
.I FILE
is something else.
.TP
.BI "trace format version " N " is not supported"
The file was written by a
.B whytime
with a different trace format.
.TP
.BI "recorded with wire ABI " X ", but this whytime uses " Y
The kernel record layout changed between the two versions of
.BR whytime ;
record the trace again with this one.
.TP
.BI "recorded on " arch ", but this whytime is built for " arch
System call numbers differ between architectures.
.TP
.B truncated
The recording ended before the observation window did: the recording
.B whytime
was killed or failed, or the attach target exited while
.B whytime
attached to it.
.PP
Trace files can contain sensitive data (argument vectors, paths, network peers
and looked-up hostnames, call stacks); see
.B TRACE FILES
in
.BR whytime (1).
.\" @OPTIONS
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was printed and every requested file written.
.TP
.B 2
.I FILE
cannot be read or replayed, an output file cannot be created or written, or
.B \-\-flamegraph
or
.B \-\-folded
was given for a trace recorded without stacks (the report is still printed),
or the command line is invalid.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record a run, then print its report again, with a timeline:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo whytime \-\-record build.wt \-\- make
whytime report build.wt \-\-timeline
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replay a trace recorded with
.BR \-\-flamegraph ,
writing the same flamegraph, its folded stacks and a Perfetto trace:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
whytime report build.wt \-\-flamegraph build.svg \-\-folded build.folded \e
    \-\-perfetto build.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract the JSON report (it goes to standard error):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
whytime report build.wt \-\-json 2>&1 |
    jq '.lines[] | {class, subject, share}'
.EE
.RE
.PP
A trace file recorded without stacks:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ \fBwhytime report demo.wt \-\-flamegraph demo.svg\fP
whytime: demo.wt: recorded without stack capture, so there is no
flamegraph to draw (record with \-\-flamegraph or \-\-folded)
wall 1.61s
\&...
$ \fBecho $?\fP
2
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whytime (1),
.BR whytime\-doctor (1)
